# The oracle images, pinned by digest.
#
# One line per oracle: <name>	<image-ref>	<version>	<what it answers>
# Tab-separated, because the last field is prose.
#
# The ref carries a tag *and* a digest. The tag is for a human reading this
# file; the digest is what runs. A tag can be moved under you and a digest
# cannot, so a green run names a set of bytes rather than a set of names.
#
# **What the version field names here is the reference, not the data**, and
# that is a departure from `unicode`'s table which is worth stating. There a
# CPython *is* a UCD version, so pinning the interpreter pins the data. Here
# the data is mostly the subject under test: the zone differential compares two
# readers of one set of TZif files, and those files are the host's. So the pin
# names the reader and the differential prints the tzdb release beside its
# numbers. ICU is the exception and goes the other way, because CLDR ships
# inside ICU - see its entry.
#
# **Each pin was first set to the version its gate was already green against**,
# deliberately, including where a newer one existed. Containerising a reference
# and raising it are two changes, and doing both at once means the next
# disagreement cannot be attributed to either. So each raise is its own commit
# with its own triage, and each entry below records what its raise changed -
# including where the answer is nothing, which is the most useful thing a raise
# can report and the easiest to leave unwritten.

# CPython, for the zone differential. Python reads the same TZif files chron
# does and the two agreeing is evidence about the files rather than about one
# reader's opinion of them.
#
# **It reads the host's zone tree, mounted read-only, not the image's.** The
# image carries its own tzdata - 2026c today, the same release the host has -
# and that coincidence is exactly the trap. The digest freezes the image's copy
# where the host's moves with Debian, so the first time the host updates, an
# unmounted oracle would begin comparing two tzdb releases and reporting the
# gap as a disagreement. Mounting makes the comparison independent of the
# image's data altogether, which is also why raising this pin can never change
# what is being compared.
python	docker.io/library/python:3.13-slim@sha256:37134a49d21d2120e4c4d73bb76f8a4ab9aef31f096f7ec2ead48c2feead4332	Python 3.13	Zone offsets, DST flags and abbreviations, over every zone the system database holds

# V8's Temporal, for RFC 9557. Temporal's string format *is* RFC 9557 - the
# `[America/New_York]` suffix was standardised for it - so this is the grammar's
# reference implementation rather than a second opinion on it.
#
# **Node 24, raised from 22 on 2026-09-25, and the raise changed nothing** -
# every count in the gate is identical, 4802 strings compared and agreed either
# way. That is the whole result and it is worth writing down, because the reason
# this entry gave for staying on 22 was wrong: it said a newer node "would
# silently retire" the measurement of what V8 rejects, when in fact V8 13.6
# rejects the critical `!` and an unknown non-critical annotation exactly as
# 12.4 did. The counts were never at risk from a raise; they are at risk from
# nobody looking.
#
# **Not Node 25**, which is newer still and unusable here: V8 14.1 lists
# `--harmony-temporal` in `--v8-options` and node accepts the flag, but
# `Temporal` is `undefined` with it, with it spelled `--harmony_temporal`, and
# with `--harmony`. The differential refuses to run rather than compare against
# nothing, which is the behaviour that turns this from a silent hole into a
# one-line message - so the ceiling on this pin is set by where Temporal is
# reachable, not by where it is newest.
node	docker.io/library/node:24-slim@sha256:0e0ff40c39bc087845bfb27465a0df4ea419520094bc35842ff83dd8cbe6f9b6	Node 24	RFC 9557 parsing: every field of every string both sides accept, and the ones only one of them does

# ICU, for the LDML formatter and parser. design.md section 8.3 makes ICU the
# *definition* of what a pattern means, so this is the one reference here whose
# disagreement is a defect rather than a reading.
#
# **This is the pin that does name data**, because CLDR ships inside ICU: what
# a pattern means is a CLDR question and the ICU release answers it. 76.1 is
# what this machine has had all along and what the seven known divergences in
# ldml_diff.py are calibrated against. `unicode` pins 78.3 for its own reason -
# it needs Unicode 17.0 - and the two libraries asking different ICUs is fine
# so long as both say so, which until 2026-09-24 neither did.
#
# **Built here, but from Debian stable rather than from source**, which is the
# other departure from `unicode`. It built 78.3 from a source tarball because
# 78.3 exists only in sid, and CONTAINERS.md section 2.6 is right that an apt
# pin into sid expires sooner than a stable one. 76.1 is Debian 13's own
# libicu-dev, and Debian 13 is stable, so that reason does not apply: the base
# is pinned by digest and the package comes from a release that does not move.
#
# Being built here, the pin is not a digest - two builds of one Dockerfile are
# not two copies of one image - so the run-time version check in oracle_env.py
# is the real guarantee. Build it with `make oracle-images`.
icu	localhost/ghoti-chron-oracle-icu:76.1	ICU 76.1	LDML pattern formatting and parsing in the root locale
